WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.060
CROWD: GOOD MORNING HANK

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:06.020
[PEOPLE SHOUTING] It's Monday! JOHN: I think it's--I think it's Tuesday. I think it's gonna be Tuesday.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.760
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday, some day at the end of October and

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.440
you just got said hello to by a bunch of extremely excited nerdfighters in Phoenix, Arizona

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.820
who are only able to say hello to you because of my awesome new camcorder!

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.220
I am so excited about my new camcorder here Hank, let me show it to you.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.580
Oh, oh wait, if I move the camcorder... I'm just gonna.. [MUMBLING]

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.880
I'm just gonna take a picture of the camcorder using the camcorder--DANG IT!!

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:34.260
[INTRO MUSIC]

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.700
Okay Hank so you might remember, in my last video I asked the nerdfighters what I could do for them

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.640
after they did all this awesome stuff for me,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.240
including getting Paper Towns on the New York Times Best Seller list

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.480
and it turns out that the nerdfighters do indeed have some requests

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.240
''I think you should do NaNoWriMo in November, which is national novel writing month!''

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.900
Unfortunately, despite getting down on my knees every night and

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.640
praying that God turn me into James Patterson, I'm not able to write a novel in a month.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.500
However, in the month of November I am considering national

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.680
'finish-your-revision-of-your-book-I-mean-seriously-come-on' month, also known as NaFRBIMSCON.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.340
The only problem with that is that November is also national 'drive-around-America-with-your-brother' month

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.340
or NaDaAWiBMo

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:14.140
So I'm definitely gonna do NaDaAWiBMo and I'm gonna try to do NaFRBIMSCON

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.340
and I apologise in advance that it's gonna be totally impossible to do NaNoWriMo.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.120
''Make sure when they make a movie of Paper Towns they don't ruin it!''

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.640
Um, okay, you're not gonna like the way I respond to that request.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.700
Uh, first, I don't  think that they can ruin a book with a movie.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.760
Like, no matter how unconvincing Sean Penn's Louisiana accent sounds,

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.760
All the King' Menace is still a great book.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.880
Also I ascribe to the belief that in order to properly adapt a book into a film

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.200
you have to radically change, like, everything about the book. Sorry.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.400
Perfecter Chemistry says:

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.240
''You can repay us by promoting literature online and also in schools everywhere!''

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.220
It's funny you should bring that up, Perfecter Chemistry,

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.840
so Hank today I was in a high school in Seattle, trying to get the kids excited about reading,

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.840
talking about books and why they still matter and blah blah blah,

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.400
and um, pretty much during my entire presentation, there was a lot of, like, laughter,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.940
and I didn't know what exactly they were laughing at, I was like 'I guess I am just one funny guy'

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:09.520
and then about forty-five minutes into my one hour talk I opened it up for questions and, like, thirty hands shot up,

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.560
which is a little bit unusual so I called on someone and they were like

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.140
''Um, yes, Mr Green,

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.340
are you aware of the fact that your zipper is like, flagrantly, almost obscenely unzipped?''

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:22.800
AAAAAAH NO I DIDN'T BUT I DID HANK!

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.760
I gave my entire speech about the importance of imagining other people complexely

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.580
with, like a massively unzipped zipper.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:38.020
''Can you just, like, give me a hug? You're just like a giant teddy bear, and I just love you.''

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.460
Did you have to say that I'm like a giant teddy bear? Couldn't you have just said that I'm like a regular teddy bear?

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:47.920
Look, I know that the Weight Watchers hasn't gone well, you try eating on the road, it hard out here for a touring author!

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.840
''It would be awesome if you went on a national campaign to make skydiving legal for minors.''

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.560
[LAUGHS] I mean, Hank, knowing what you do about me,

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.040
can you imagine a campaign that I am less likely to go on?

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.540
I don't think skydiving should be legal for anyone!

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.600
It's crazy! You shouldn't jump out of a plane, planes are for staying in!

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.600
''Make my Walmart get Paper Towns!''

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.600
It's actually an exceptionally bad idea to buy your books at Walmart,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.880
because if everyone buys their books at Walmart then

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.880
we're looking at a future when about thirty to fifty books will be published each year,

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.920
which will would have kind of a chilling effect on American cultural discourse,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.100
plus it would put me out of a job because Walmart thinks my books are too dirty to stock.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.060
So Hank it was starting to look like there was nothing the nerdfighters wanted me to do,

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:28.100
that I could do for them.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.100
I mean, I can't do NaNoWriMo because I don't have time,

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.660
my conscience won't allow me to advocate for minors skydiving because I think it's a terrible idea,

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.860
and I can't get my books into Walmart because I'm too dirty!

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.700
But then finally, Hank, I saw a fulfillable request!

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.180
''Could you record yourself doing your happy dance for us in eighteen different states?''

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.120
YES. I. CAN!

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.420
My zipper is unzipped!

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:52.760
DANG IT!

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.820
Hank, I'll see you in just a few minutes at the amazing Seattle Public Library!

